1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,144
(Q21（单选）) I've been reading your personal statement, PAUL.

2
00:00:02,144 --> 00:00:05,804
First, let's talk about your work experience in South America.

3
00:00:05,804 --> 00:00:09,343
What took you there? Was it to gain more fluency in Spanish?

4
00:00:09,343 --> 00:00:17,441
Well, as I'm combining Spanish with Latin American studies, my main idea was to find out more about the way people lived there.

5
00:00:17,441 --> 00:00:20,340
My spoken Spanish was already pretty good in fact.

6
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:23,010
So you weren't too worried about language barriers?

7
00:00:23,010 --> 00:00:28,272
No. In fact, I ended up teaching English there, although that wasn't my original choice of work.

8
00:00:28,272 --> 00:00:31,290
I see. How did you find out about all this?

9
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:35,407
I found an agency that runs all kinds of voluntary projects in South America.

10
00:00:35,407 --> 00:00:37,070
What kind of work?

11
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:39,353
Well, there were several possibilities.

12
00:00:39,353 --> 00:00:41,641
You mean construction? Engineering work?

13
00:00:41,641 --> 00:00:45,031
Yes, getting involved in building projects was an option.

14
00:00:45,031 --> 00:00:53,833
Then there was tourism - taking tourists for walks around the volcanoes - which I actually chose to do, and then there was work with local farmers.

15
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:57,535
[更换志愿项目] But you didn't continue with that project. Why not?

16
00:00:57,535 --> 00:01:01,192
Because I never really knew whether I'd be needed or not.

17
00:01:01,192 --> 00:01:09,826
I'd thought it might be difficult physically, but I was certainly fit enough.., no,I wanted to do something that had more of a proper structure to it, I suppose.

18
00:01:09,826 --> 00:01:12,052
I get de-motivated otherwise.

19
00:01:12,052 --> 00:01:14,932
What do you think you learned from your experience?

20
00:01:14,932 --> 00:01:17,857
It must have been a great opportunity to examine community life.

21
00:01:17,857 --> 00:01:22,016
Yes, but it was difficult at first to be accepted by the locals.

22
00:01:22,016 --> 00:01:30,385
It was a very remote village and some of them were reluctant to speak to me - although they were always interested in my clothes and how much I'd had to pay for them.

23
00:01:30,385 --> 00:01:31,759
Well, that's understandable.

24
00:01:31,759 --> 00:01:34,076
Yes, but things soon improved.

25
00:01:34,076 --> 00:01:41,425
What struck me was that when people became more comfortable with me and less suspicious, we really connected with each other in a meaningful way.

26
00:01:41,425 --> 00:01:42,863
You made good friends?

27
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:45,556
Yes, with two of the families in particular.

28
00:01:45,556 --> 00:01:49,457
[项目管理与生活条件] Good. What about management. Did you have a project manager?

29
00:01:49,457 --> 00:01:52,879
Yes and he gave me lots of advice and guidance.

30
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:54,890
And was he good at managing too?

31
00:01:54,890 --> 00:02:03,010
That wasn't his strong point! I think he was often more interested in the academic side of things than filing reports.

32
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:04,761
He was a bit of a dreamer.

33
00:02:04,761 --> 00:02:07,427
And did you have a contract?

34
00:02:07,427 --> 00:02:10,340
I had to stay for a minimum of three months.

35
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:15,994
My parents were surprised when I asked to stay longer - six months in the end. I was so happy there.

36
00:02:15,994 --> 00:02:21,696
And did anything on the administration side of things surprise you? What was the food and lodging like?

37
00:02:21,696 --> 00:02:27,754
Simple... but there was plenty to eat and I only paid seven dollars a day for that which was amazing really.

38
00:02:27,754 --> 00:02:30,816
And they gave me all the equipment I needed.., even a laptop.

39
00:02:30,816 --> 00:02:32,520
You didn't expect that then?

40
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:33,432
No.

41
00:02:33,432 --> 00:02:36,118
Well, I'll look forward to hearing more.

42
00:02:36,118 --> 00:02:42,343
(Q27（Matching）) But now let's look at these modules. You'll need to strat thinking about which ones you will definitely want to study.

43
00:02:42,343 --> 00:02:45,935
The first one here is Gender Studies in Latin America.

44
00:02:45,935 --> 00:02:46,603
Mmm...

45
00:02:46,603 --> 00:02:51,746
It looks at how gender analysis is reconfiguring civil society in Latin America.

46
00:02:51,746 --> 00:03:04,025
[Q27答案句： C， he won't do ➡️ 和公共xx有关， Paul说如果不是（if it was 虚拟），那他就做， 意思是， 这个他不会做。 拒绝。] 女生：Women are increasingly occupying positions in government and in other elected leadership positions in Latin America. I think you'd find it interesting.Paul：If it was to do with people in the villages rather than those in the public sphere, I would.

47
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,000
⭐

48
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:08,179
(Q28) Okay. What about Second Language Acquisition?

49
00:03:08,179 --> 00:03:10,053
Do you think I'd find that useful?

50
00:03:10,053 --> 00:03:18,142
[Q28答案句：A， will do, 因为 put that down as a definite] 女生：Well, you've had some practical experience in the field, I think it would be.Paul： I hadn't thought about that. I'll put that down as a definite, then.

51
